# ==== T-FMT-C · expression-position match — suite-layout form preservation
#
# AXIS: {case arm, else arm} x {inline, next-line 1 stmt, next-line N stmts}
#       = 6 cells. Every cell is spelled in the form `gg fmt` must PRODUCE,
#       so the assertion is "fmt is a FIXPOINT on this file".
#
# RED pre-fix (recorded 2026-08-11 on the pre-fix binary):
#   * else/next-1  — `else:` + next-line `20` COLLAPSED to `else: 20`
#                    (`format_arm_body`'s stmts.len() <= 1 shape test).
#   * else/next-N  — body kept, but a spurious BLANK line appeared after
#                    the enclosing var-decl (the double-newline class).
#   * all 6 hosts  — the same spurious blank; an expression-position suite's
#                    last stmt newline-terminated, then the enclosing
#                    `Stmt::VarDecl` wrote its own newline.
# GREEN pre-fix / BREAK-AND-WATCH: the three `case` cells preserve layout
#   today. To see them RED, make `format_match_arm` ignore `Block.layout`
#   (always take the inline branch) — the next-line case bodies collapse.

int case_inline(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1: 10
        else: 20
    return r

int case_next_one(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1:
            10
        else:
            20
    return r

int case_next_many(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1:
            int t = x + 1
            t * 2
        else:
            int u = x - 1
            u * 3
    return r

# Mixed: an inline `case` beside a next-line `else` — the two arms of ONE
# construct must each keep the form its author spelled (the owner-reported
# "inconsistent within one construct" symptom).
int mixed_forms(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1: 10
        else:
            int u = x - 1
            u * 3
    return r

void main():
    print(case_inline(1))
    print(case_next_one(1))
    print(case_next_many(1))
    print(mixed_forms(1))
